# =============================================================================
# Rahfi's Portfolio: environment reference
# =============================================================================
# Copy to `.env` and fill in the real values by hand. `.env` is gitignored and is
# never read, printed, or copied by anyone, including an AI assistant, per
# docs/rules/env.rules.md. Only this example file is ever opened.
#
# Keep this file in step with the code: a new variable is added here in the same
# commit that introduces it, so the example never falls behind what runs.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ PREFIX WARNING
# Anything prefixed NEXT_PUBLIC_ is compiled into the JavaScript bundle the
# browser downloads. It is public the moment it ships. Never move a secret
# behind that prefix to "make it work" on the client. Move the call to the
# server instead. See docs/rules/secret.rules.md.
# =============================================================================


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AI assistant: SECRET
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Read only inside a server action (src/app/actions.ts). Never reaches the browser.
# Obtain from: https://aistudio.google.com/apikey
GEMINI_API_KEY=your_gemini_api_key_here


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GitHub statistics: SECRET
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Read only inside the API route (src/app/api/github/stats/route.ts).
# A fine-grained token with PUBLIC REPOSITORY READ ONLY is sufficient. Grant no
# write scope: this token only counts repositories and lists recent ones.
# Obtain from: https://github.com/settings/tokens
GITHUB_TOKEN=your_github_token_here


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Contact form delivery: SECRET
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Read only inside a server action. GMAIL_APP_PASSWORD is an App Password, not
# your account password: generate one with 2FA enabled and revoke it if leaked.
# Obtain from: https://myaccount.google.com/apppasswords
GMAIL_USER=your_email@example.com
GMAIL_APP_PASSWORD=your_16_character_app_password


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Bot protection: one secret, one public
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The secret key verifies a token server-side and must never ship to the client.
# The site key is designed to be public; it identifies the site to Google.
# Obtain both from: https://www.google.com/recaptcha/admin/create  (reCAPTCHA v3)
RECAPTCHA_SECRET_KEY=your_recaptcha_secret_key_here
NEXT_PUBLIC_RECAPTCHA_SITE_KEY=your_recaptcha_site_key_here


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Sanity CMS: public
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Project id and dataset are public identifiers; the dataset itself is read via
# the public API. Use a SEPARATE dataset for preview if you do not want draft
# content served from a production deployment.
# Obtain from: https://www.sanity.io/manage
NEXT_PUBLIC_SANITY_PROJECT_ID=your_sanity_project_id
NEXT_PUBLIC_SANITY_DATASET=production
NEXT_PUBLIC_SANITY_API_VERSION=2025-12-01

# A WRITE token. SECRET. Not needed to read: the client in
# src/sanity/lib/client.ts sends no token and reads through the public API, which
# is why the blog, the projects and the certificates all render without one.
#
# It IS needed for one thing: the button on /admin that copies the projects and
# certificates out of src/data/resume.tsx into Sanity, the first time. After that
# migration the site reads them from Sanity and this token can be revoked, unless
# you add something else that writes.
#
# The same dataset also holds the consulting site's engagements. There is one
# studio, at /studio here, and consulting redirects its own /studio to it.
#
# Get one at https://www.sanity.io/manage -> your project -> API -> Tokens ->
# Add API token. Give it the LOWEST permission that does the job: "Viewer" reads,
# "Editor" writes, "Deploy Studio" is for the studio only. The value is shown
# exactly once; if you lose it, revoke it and issue a new one rather than hunting.
#
# Two things not to do with it:
#   - Never prefix it NEXT_PUBLIC_. A write token in the browser bundle is an open
#     write endpoint to your dataset for anyone who opens devtools.
#   - Never add it to the existing `client` in src/sanity/lib/client.ts. That module
#     is imported by src/data/blog.ts, and the moment anything imports it from a
#     client component the token ships with it. Build a separate client inside the
#     server route that needs it.
SANITY_API_WRITE_TOKEN=your_sanity_write_token_here


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Supabase: visitor analytics
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The anon key is public BY DESIGN. It is not a secret, and row-level security is
# what protects the data behind it. The policies live in
# supabase/migrations/. If those policies are wrong, this key is a write endpoint
# for the public internet, so review them before deploying.
#
# Never put a SERVICE ROLE key in a NEXT_PUBLIC_ variable. It bypasses every
# policy. If server-side writes are needed, add SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY
# without the prefix and read it only from a server action or route handler.
# Obtain from: Supabase dashboard > Project Settings > API
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://your_project_ref.supabase.co
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=your_supabase_anon_key_here


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CMS access: SECRET
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The single address permitted to sign in. Checked on the server on every request,
# not only at sign-in, so a magic link mailed to any other address authenticates
# nobody. If this is unset the CMS is CLOSED, not open, because unconfigured must never
# mean unlocked.
CMS_OWNER_EMAIL=you@example.com


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Google Cloud Storage: the most dangerous secret in this project
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A service-account key is a standing credential against your bucket. Unlike an API
# key it is not scoped by any per-request check, so treat it accordingly:
#
#   - Scope the service account to ONE bucket with object permissions only
#     (roles/storage.objectAdmin). Never grant bucket administration and never a
#     project-wide role: an object-scoped key that leaks costs the bucket's
#     contents, a project-scoped one costs the project.
#   - Never give it a NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix. It is read only in src/lib/gcs.ts, which
#     is server-only, and it must never be compiled into the browser bundle.
#   - Rotate it if it is ever pasted anywhere it should not have been, including a
#     chat window, a log, or a screenshot.
#
# Base64-encode the WHOLE key JSON file. Base64 because the PEM inside contains
# newlines, and a raw paste into a hosting provider's environment UI mangles them,
# the single most common cause of an "invalid PEM" at deploy time.
#
#   Linux/macOS:  base64 -w0 key.json
#   PowerShell:   [Convert]::ToBase64String([IO.File]::ReadAllBytes("key.json"))
GCS_PROJECT_ID=your_gcp_project_id
GCS_BUCKET=your_bucket_name
GCS_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_KEY=paste_the_base64_encoded_key_json_here

# Upload ceiling in bytes. Enforced on the actual byte length, not on a declared
# Content-Length, which the uploader controls. Default 10485760 (10 MB).
MAX_UPLOAD_BYTES=10485760
